flowchart TD

  subgraph L0["Level 0 · Question"]
    Q["<b>Why do global trade imbalances persist,<br/>and why could they worsen before improving?</b><br/><i>Brad Setser, CFR, Sept 2019</i>"]
  end

  subgraph L1["Level 1 · Priors & assumptions"]
    P1["<b>Global surpluses require matching deficits</b><br/>If large economies save externally, another economy must absorb the surplus"]
    P2["<b>Fiscal policy shapes external balances</b><br/>Weak domestic demand tends to reduce imports and raise surpluses"]
    P3["<b>Excess saving generates surplus pressure</b><br/>Saving above domestic investment pushes capital and demand abroad"]
  end

  subgraph L2["Level 2 · Evidence"]
    D1["<b>Europe and East Asia run large surpluses</b><br/>Together they roughly mirror the U.S. manufacturing deficit"]
    D2["<b>Europe remains fiscally tight</b><br/>Germany and other surplus economies run restrictive demand policies"]
    D3["<b>Asian surplus economies restrain demand/currencies</b><br/>Korea, Taiwan, Singapore, and Thailand remain export oriented"]
    D4["<b>China has a surplus despite huge fiscal support</b><br/>Current account ~$200–250B with augmented deficit near 12% of GDP"]
    D5["<b>China still saves ~44% of GDP</b><br/>East Asian aggregate saving remains close to 40%"]
    D6["<b>China's imports weakened in 2019</b><br/>Modest stimulus failed to offset tightening and the trade war"]
    D7["<b>U.S. net external debt ~45% of GDP</b><br/>Large relative to the country's limited export base"]
  end

  subgraph L3["Level 3 · Mechanisms"]
    M1["<b>Fiscal restraint suppresses domestic demand</b><br/>Lower imports and excess saving generate European surpluses"]
    M2["<b>Structural high saving generates surplus pressure</b><br/>China saves more than its economy can sustainably absorb"]
    M3["<b>Weak investment and imports raise China's surplus</b><br/>Less domestic absorption pushes the external balance upward"]
    M4["<b>Foreign surpluses require deficit absorption</b><br/>North America takes the other side through external deficits and debt"]
  end

  subgraph L4["Level 4 · Intermediate claims"]
    C1["<b>Europe's imbalance is mainly a demand problem</b><br/>Looser fiscal policy could directly raise domestic absorption"]
    C2["<b>East Asia's imbalance is structurally harder</b><br/>China's underlying problem is exceptionally high saving"]
    C3["<b>China's current adjustment is fragile</b><br/>Large public investment prevents an even larger external surplus"]
    C4["<b>The U.S. cannot absorb surpluses forever</b><br/>Its external debt stock is already large relative to exports"]
  end

  subgraph L5["Level 5 · Conclusion"]
    K["<b>Main claim: global imbalances could worsen first</b><br/>Europe still lacks demand and China's excess-saving problem remains;<br/>the U.S. cannot indefinitely absorb the resulting surpluses"]
  end

  subgraph L6["Level 6 · Dialectic"]
    X["<b>U.S.-adjustment alternative</b><br/>Tighter U.S. fiscal policy would also reduce the American trade deficit"]
    R1["<b>Japan exception</b><br/>Japan runs a surplus despite relatively loose fiscal policy"]
    QF1["<b>U.S. sustainability caveat</b><br/>The current flow deficit is not obviously unsustainable today"]
    QF2["<b>China adjustment caveat</b><br/>Fiscal and credit stimulus currently absorbs much of China's excess saving"]
  end

  P2 --> M1
  D2 --> M1
  M1 --> C1

  P3 --> M2
  D4 --> M2
  D5 --> M2
  M2 --> C2

  D6 --> M3
  M2 --> M3
  M3 --> C3

  P1 --> M4
